# HIL fault injection

Never lower a trip threshold to force a fault. Inject a realistic input and
exercise the real sensing path.

- Voltage faults: command bus or battery voltage in the Typhoon model.
- Current faults: current is computed inside the model, not exposed as an
 input. Find the model input that drives it. If none exists, say so and stop.
 Do not fall back to a lowered threshold.
- Software or debug faults with no sensing path use their debug CAN command.

Assert the fault class the firmware should show. Never loosen an assert to
match what the bench did. A latch fault goes to Faulted, an autoclear fault
goes to Standby, an info fault changes no state.

# Suspected firmware bugs

When the bench shows behavior that looks wrong, open a Jira ticket even at
low confidence. Visibility beats waiting for proof.

Investigations live in tickets, not in Slack threads or side chats. Draft the
ticket in chat for Spencer to post, in declarative statements.

# Ticket documentation

Update the ticket as the work moves, not once implementation is done. Edit
the description, not a comment. Say what the firmware does, step by step, in
words its owner reads without the test code. Before a ticket closes, it
records:

- Fault injection method
- Trip thresholds
- Clear thresholds
- Timing requirements
- Units
- Key implementation choices
- Assumptions or limitations used during testing

# Firmware changes

A firmware change is exercised on HIL before it reaches validation. The order
is: developer bench run, PR smoke tier, nightly regression.
